  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/f/f9/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_115.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A haggard man is shown in India seeking \"the Ancient One,\" seeking a \"magic healing power.\"&nbsp; He encounters an old man who claims to be the one he's seeking but who also indicates that he bestows his power only on those <i>worthy</i> of it.&nbsp; After probing the traveler's mind, the Ancient One learns that he is Stephen Strange, a former famous surgeon who cared only about money, refusing to help any who could not afford his services, turning down every opportunity to give back to mankind unless he was compensated for his time.\n\nBut an auto accident changed all that in seconds.&nbsp; Though his life was spared, the accident caused irreparable damage to the nerves in his hands, rendering them shaky and unsuitable for surgery.&nbsp; It was a devastating blow and threatened to spell the end of his career.&nbsp; In bitterness, he spurned many offers to become the assistant of others, refusing to stoop so low as to work for another, even as a consultant.&nbsp; He withdrew from society, losing track of time, eventually becoming little more than a drifter.\n\nBut one day, while walking on a dock, he heard rumors of an \"Ancient One,\" whose power to cure was based on magic.&nbsp; With nothing to lose, Dr. Strange set out to find this \"Ancient One,\" which led him finally here, months later, to India... and into the presence of the Ancient One himself.\n\nBut the old man refuses to heal him, due to his selfish motives, unless he stays and studies with him.&nbsp; Only then might he find the cure he seeks.&nbsp; But to Strange, this only seems to confirm that the old man is a fraud, and he prepares to depart in anger and disappointment.\n\nBut the sudden arrival of a snowstorm prevents his departure, and he's forced to accept the hospitality of the old man, who offers him a room until the surrounding area thaws enough for travel.&nbsp; Entering the elaborate dwelling, he is shown to his room by another pupil, a \"creepy-looking character\" named Mordo.\n\nThe snow does not immediately melt, and Strange notes that Mordo does little else but study scrolls and recite dirges.&nbsp; When he ventures to the Ancient One's quarters to ask how long it takes for snow to melt here, he observes the old man asleep and suddenly attacked by the \"Vapors of Valtorr!\"&nbsp; Strange then witnesses the Ancient One fighting off a mystical attack, only then coming to believe that magic is real.&nbsp; The Ancient One admits he is constantly beset by the forces of evil and indicates that he's seeking a successor, as these forces can never be allowed to come to Earth.\n\nBut Strange does not truly believe until he happens to see Mordo offering an incense to someone named \"Dormammu.\"&nbsp; He realizes that the attack came from Mordo.&nbsp; The Ancient One's <i>own pupil</i> is trying to kill him!\n\nSeeing that Dr. Strange has spotted him, Mordo admits that he is <i>indeed</i> trying to slay his master, in order to become \"master of black magic.\"&nbsp; But since he believes he is more powerful than the \"unknowing Western dog,\" he casts a spell on Strange that prevents him from informing the Ancient One of anything that he's learned.&nbsp; Strange is blocked from saying anything and finally realizes that the magic here is real.\n\nHe tries to warn the Ancient One, but smug Mordo, standing right there, knows he cannot reveal the truth.&nbsp; But he can still attempt to learn magic from the Ancient One so that he can \"battle Mordo with his own weapons!\"\n\nThe Ancient One is pleased that Strange has finally seen the light and frees him from Mordo's spell, which of course he knew about, for \"the pupil can have no secrets from his master!\"&nbsp; Despite the constant threat, the Ancient One nevertheless prefers to keep Mordo close by, to more easily observe his plans and stop them.\n\nBut he tells Strange that \"one day, my son, when I am gone, it will be <i>your</i> task to battle Mordo... to the finish!\"\n\nDr. Strange then learns the ways of the mystic arts over the next weeks, months, and years.&nbsp; Finally, his life takes on a new meaning, and he stands ready for the epic battles ahead -- ready at last to battle Mordo when at last he strikes -- as \"Dr. Strange, master of black magic!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "The origin story of Dr. Strange may have come <b><i>late</i></b> (seeing as there had already been three previous adventures published in this title about the character), but nevertheless, [SPAN: severity=success; text=kudos to Stan and Steve for finally serving up a backstory] for Stephen Strange, former physician-turned-magician, all due to an accident that ultimately led him to seek out this \"Ancient One\" in India.\n\n[ASSET: left | StrangeTheBum | width=14%] Brief as it was, it was a [SPAN: severity=success; text=solid and inventive origin tale], unlike any that Marvel had done before, as it involved nothing like cosmic rays, radioactivity, or even a freak occurrence.&nbsp; It was as simple as an auto accident which spelled the end of a selfish surgeon's career, one who had to be humbled enough to seek out what he viewed as an impossible cure, ultimately leading him to the Ancient One... <i>and</i> his evil pupil Mordo.\n[BR]\n[BR] The bleak situation of Stephen Strange was well-done (though showing him as a shiftless bum might have been a <i>little</i> over the top).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | ParlorTricks | width=21%] I liked the fact that when Strange comes face-to-face with the Ancient One, he initially turns his back, only to have his exit blocked by a sudden snowstorm and is forced to take shelter with the old man, who you can tell<i> </i>is almost <i>winking</i> at the notion that <i>of course</i>, no man of the Western world could <i>possibly</i> believe in magic.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"It would be... unseemly!\"]\n[BR] You can almost see the Ancient One smirking behind Strange's back, <i>heh</i>.\n\n[ASSET: left | EvilMordo | width=13%] It didn't take long before Strange found out that magic is as real as advertised, and even <i>worse</i> was the fact that the Ancient One's <i>own pupil</i> was gunning to take his place!\n[BR]\n[BR] This might have landed a bit harder had it not been for the fact that readers had already been introduced to the evil Baron Mordo in earlier issues, but it still comes with <i>some</i> degree of wonder.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=Does the Ancient One know that his own student is actively trying to bump him off?]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | WeakWesternDog | width=11%] And when Strange tries to interfere, he learns the hard way that magic can be used for both good [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=and evil], slapped with a spell that prevents him from revealing to the Ancient One what he's learned.\n[BR]\n[BR] Discovering this level of duplicity on the part of the Ancient One's own student is what finally leads to Strange's \"come to Jesus\" moment, and he crawls back to the old man, tail between his legs, begging to learn the mystic secrets after all.\n\n[ASSET: left | OfferAccepted | width=21%] You get the sense that the Ancient One guessed all along that Strange would decide to fight back against Mordo by learning the \"secrets of black magic\" that would allow him to one day fight fire with fire.\n[BR]\n[BR] And <i>naturally</i>, the Ancient One turns out to know all about Mordo, electing to practice the old adage:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Keep your friends close, and your enemies <i>closer</i>!\"]\n\nIt's a fitting closeout for the origin story that catapults Stephen Strange into an entirely new world as \"master of black magic.\"\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAs for the artwork, Steve Ditko's quirky style works for this character every bit as much as it does for Spider-Man, and I find that I actually like it (though I am hardly a Ditko fan).&nbsp; The rays that emanate like lightning bolts from the various characters' hands are oddly fascinating, [DECODER_RING: And they remind me of Spider-Man's early encounters with Electro.] and the mists that always seem to be present around these mysterious figures seem fully in character.\n\nIt's not a genre I ordinarily gravitate toward, but the surroundings for it seem well-done.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe splash page acknowledges that [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=whoops!] this probably should have been done a few issues earlier.&nbsp; But the ordering snafu doesn't change the fact that this was the [SPAN: severity=success; text=best <i>Dr. Strange</i> outing thus far.]\n\nThat being said, since this was clearly an important story, you'd think there might have been at least <i>some</i> notice of it on the cover, but there was <i>literally</i> no mention of Dr. Strange anywhere to be found.&nbsp; I <i>understand</i> that showing Sandman against the Human Torch was its own selling point, in and of itself, but... [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold-italic; text=nothing at all?]\n\nThat's pretty harsh if you ask me!&nbsp; (I actually owned a copy of this book, and it wasn't until I actually opened it and paged through it that I realized what <i>else</i> was special about it.)\n\nMy biggest criticism of the story isn't about the origin itself -- that much at least makes perfect sense -- but in the way the reader is never given much idea about Mordo's motivations, which I don't believe were clearly defined.&nbsp; He makes it known that he wants to be the \"only master of black magic,\" but I still have to ask...\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Why</i>?&nbsp; What does it matter to you if the Ancient One is out there?]\n\nWe can only assume that his evil plans would somehow be thwarted if the Ancient One was still around, and he appeals to this mysterious [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"Dormammu\"] to accept his offering.&nbsp; Is there an expectation that this offering will convince whoever-this-is to grant him some power that will allow him to overwhelm his master?\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=The story never says], and even seems unconcerned with providing readers more details.\n\nThis could be for as innocent a reason as lack of space, but unless there is a plan to augment this, I still walk away from the story scratching my head a bit, especially in the aftermath of Strange being accepted as yet <i>another</i> pupil who we're told was there for <b><i>years</i></b>.\n\nAre we to believe that in <i>all that time</i>, there was no competition between Strange and Mordo?\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=That seems hard to believe.]\n\nDespite this, and despite the fact that I continue to regard the character of Dr. Strange with a general feeling of \"disinterest,\" I did at least read this tale with some bit of fascination, crammed though it was within a mere 8 pages.\n\nIt was rushed -- <b><i>absolutely</i></b> -- and was no great work of fiction in the end, but no reader can deny that it was... [SPAN: severity=success; text=unique, <i>especially</i> as an origin story] and it is primarily for that reason that it rates as high as it does.\n\nI sincerely hope that all upcoming Dr. Strange stories will at least be <i>this</i> good in the months ahead.",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}